each year science magazines editors and  new staff select a breakthrough of the  year  a novel finding that makes headway in  solving a long-standing problem and is  of interest to a wide array of people in  this video a few of our news writers  provide a brief glimpse of both the 2011  breakthrough and the 9 runners-up this  last year marks a turning point in HIV  prevention AIDS surface 31 years ago and  in the intervening years there have been  very few prevention efforts that use a  biomedical intervention that have proven  themselves to work so we've relied on  things like condoms and behavior change  and we know the limitations of those now  it's been shown if you treat somebody  with antiretroviral drugs you lower  their risk of transmitting to someone by  96% combining that with other biomedical  prevention interventions that have  proven themselves as well as the  behavioral in condoms has led to a  concept now that we can actually end the  epidemic in locales if not the entire  world in time this is not a vaccine a  vaccine would be a there's a great  desire to have something that you give  somebody three times and they're  protected for life this requires  treatment for decades on end it's  complicated it's costly but it's  something that the world can do tomorrow  and that has changed the level of hope  in a way that's profound  for decades astronomers have been  confused about where the most common  types of meteorites that follow the  earth come from they come from the  asteroid belt but when they looked out  into the asteroid belt  the most common asteroids they saw  didn't look like the meteorites they had  in their hands in 2011 Japanese  scientists were able to analyze tiny  bits of the asteroid Itokawa that the  Hayabusa spacecraft had returned to  Earth and with a close look they could  see that the asteroid has the same  chemical and mineralogical composition  as the most common meteorites found on  earth so that told them that the  distinctive reddened color of the  asteroids was just an overlay it had  been fooling them but it wasn't easy the  return of the Hayabusa spacecraft was  complicated by mechanical failures  need to fall back on one backup after  another but this year the decades-old  conundrum of where the most common  meteorites come from has been resolved  last year a flurry of new studies came  out that showed as modern humans the  first members of Homo sapiens came out  of Africa sometime in the last hundred  thousand years they encountered archaic  people in more than one place and in a  bread with them and so a whole group of  new studies came out that tried to find  who still carries the DNA from these  encounters of the prehistoric kind they  found DNA in Europeans and Asian's that  show that they interbred the modern  humans interbred with Neanderthals but  they also found that Melanesians  Australian Aborigines and Negrete owes  and the Philippines carried DNA from a  new kind of human that was discovered in  Siberia that lived more than thirty  thousand years ago  so it's very interesting we carry a  small amount of DNA between say  - and 10% of our DNA from archaic people  that we interact with on at least three  different continents this is significant  because it's changing our models of  modern human origins it turns out that  as Homo sapiens came out of Africa they  didn't completely replace the akai  archaic people we still carry some of  their DNA in our veins  this year researchers in Japan gave us  our first full-scale atomic look at a  protein complex called photosystem ii so  photosystem ii is the protein that  plants used to split water into hydrogen  and oxygen and chemists would very much  like to do the same thing to generate  molecular hydrogen that can be used as  an alternative fuel that wouldn't have  any co2 or other carbon compounds that  would be greenhouse gases and so what's  amazing about the catalyst that is that  the core of photosystem ii is it is  virtually unchanged by billions of years  of evolution which tells it tells us  that it is exquisitely tuned to doing  its job and chemists are now trying to  learn those exact same lessons to be  able to use those insights for  alternative energy so a better  understanding of how plants do this  which the structure does provide could  really help that effort so this year  astronomers found something very unusual  they found two clouds of gas twelve  billion light-years away that are made  almost entirely of hydrogen they have no  elements in them that are heavier which  is very surprising because two billion  years after the Big Bang which is the  era when these two gas clouds existed  the universe was supposed to have been a  very violent place and all of the gases  in the universe were supposed to have  been mixed up amongst themselves so that  no pockets of the universe were supposed  to be left uncontaminated by heavier  elements made in the hearts of stars the  other find along the same lines with the  discovery of a star  that again seems to be made entirely of  hydrogen and helium and has no elements  heavier than those together these finds  suggests that the chemical evolution of  the universe had more twists and turns  than astronomers had taught the  microbiome is the community of organisms  that live in and on our bodies in over  the last few years we've really come to  appreciate how diverse those communities  are so we decided that microbiome  research deserved runner-up status  because there were several key papers  that came out in 2011 one has to do with  discerning a pattern in the complexity  of the gut bacteria we now think we can  divide these communities into three  categories these categories correlate  with long-term diet and are named  according to the dominant bacteria in  them we also advanced our understanding  of the role of microbiome in cancer in  immune system function and in metabolic  diseases and together all this work  really enhanced our appreciation of just  how important these microbial  communities are to our health and  well-being  so 2011 saw the publication of the first  partial results of a phase 3 clinical  trial of a malaria vaccine this was a  huge study involving more than 15,000  young children in seven countries in  Africa all the way from Ghana to  Mozambique now malaria vaccine  development has been an incredibly  frustrating difficult field with lots of  disappointments and this was the first  phase free study with some real degree  of success  about half of the children aged between  5 and 17 months were protected from  severe malaria which can be fatal if you  get it that's still not a terrific  result most vaccines perform much better  than that and there are other questions  about this vaccine as well we don't know  how long the protection will last if it  wanes after one or two years we also  don't know how much this vaccine will  costs but it will be affordable for the  countries that really need it or who  will pay the bills but still given given  the long and frustrating history it's a  start and researchers can build on this  vaccine to design maybe others that that  do better so despite all the  shortcomings we think this is a  runner-up for breakthrough of the year  so for the past 15 years astronomers  have been discovering all sorts of  exoplanets or planets outside the solar  system these are strange beasts some are  as dense as lead some as light as  styrofoam and this year they've  continued to discover several weird  exoplanets as well as weird exoplanetary  systems in February they discovered a  system with six planets five of which  are closer to their parent star than  mercury is to the Sun that is very  surprising because you don't expect so  many planets to be bunched up together  so close to the parent star on the other  extreme astronomers discovered a cluster  of free-floating planets that  appeared to have come unhinged from  their parent star in fact there was no  parent star in sight  and so these discoveries are showing  that planetary systems have a chaotic  history of violent history that  researchers are only now beginning to  understand  so zeolites are a family of porous  minerals that we've known about for over  250 years and they've extremely useful  in modern society we use them as  catalysts as for breaking apart things  like the long hydrocarbon chains in oil  into the shorter ones that are in  gasoline that are more volatile they are  used to as filters for purifying air  purifying water now the problem was the  eliza's that in the past they've tended  to have extremely small pores and that's  limited their usefulness what happened  this year was that researchers in Korea  and then another group in France in  Germany came up with way different ways  to make zeolites without only the small  pore sizes but also larger ones which  could make them as useful catalysts for  and filters for a wider variety of  compounds researchers in Minnesota also  reported a new way to make ultra-thin  zeolite membranes which could be very  useful as cheaper more effective filters  and catalysts as well so I think about  2011 taught us is that zeolite research  still has a lot of potential head  scientists studying cells in the  laboratory have known for a long time  that those cells will slowly stop  dividing and enter a period of old age  or what they call senescence they've  also learned more recently that  senescence cells build up in certain  tissues and may contribute to  age-related symptoms such as cataracts  the wrinkling of skin even the muscle  weakness that comes to many elderly but  they haven't been able to prove that the  senescence cells caused those symptoms  this year at the Mayo Clinic in  Minnesota a team actually showed that  for the first  they genetically engineered mice so that  if given a drug their senescence cells  would be eliminated those mice didn't  live longer but they did have a  healthier life they warded off some of  those age-related symptoms if the drug  was given to middle-aged mice they also  benefited not clear that we can target  senescence cells and people but if we  can figure out a way we might be able to  live healthier if not longer
 

